# stats-retention.env — the one secret server/stats-retention.sh needs.
#
# Install:  sudo cp server/stats-retention.env.example /etc/stats-retention.env
#           sudo chown root:root /etc/stats-retention.env && sudo chmod 600 /etc/stats-retention.env
#           sudo nano /etc/stats-retention.env   # fill in the real value
#
# ⛔ SUPABASE_DB_URL IS THE POSTGRES SUPERUSER (same credential
# server/supabase-backup.env already installs — reuse that value verbatim,
# do not create a second password). It bypasses every RLS policy over real
# student data (.claude/rules/security.md). This file must be root-owned,
# mode 600, and never copied to a laptop, a VITE_ var, or anything under src/.
#
# Why a direct psql connection and not a PostgREST call: prune_analytics(),
# prune_claude_usage_samples() and prune_notify_log() are all `security
# definer` functions REVOKED from anon/authenticated/public (supabase/
# migrations/0055, 0065, 0231) — intentionally uncallable from the app, the
# anon key or even a service_role REST call. Only a direct Postgres
# connection authenticated as an owner/superuser role can run them, which is
# exactly what SUPABASE_DB_URL is.
#
# See server/supabase-backup.env.example for the direct-host-vs-pooler
# (IPv6) note — it applies identically here, unverified from this agent (no
# VM access, no production DB credential).
SUPABASE_DB_URL=postgresql://postgres:CHANGE_ME@db.CHANGE_ME.supabase.co:5432/postgres?sslmode=require

# Retention windows (defaults shown; all optional).
# ANALYTICS_RETAIN_DAYS=90
# CLAUDE_RETAIN_WEEKS=8
# NOTIFY_LOG_RETAIN_DAYS=30
